---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Policy"
  description="How content on The Layer Line is researched, written, verified, and updated — including how AI is used in the process."
  path="/editorial-policy/"
>
  <div class="container prose-page">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Editorial Policy', href: '/editorial-policy/' }]} />
    <header class="prose-page__header">
      <h1>How we research, write, and correct content</h1>
      <p class="prose-page__lead">Last updated September 1, 2026.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-page__body">
      <h2>What this site covers</h2>
      <p>
        The Layer Line publishes guides about desktop FDM (fused deposition modeling) 3D printing: print quality
        troubleshooting, calibration, and material behavior. We don't cover resin (SLA/DLP) printing, industrial
        additive manufacturing, or specific printer reviews.
      </p>

      <h2>Research and sourcing</h2>
      <p>
        Guides are built primarily from well-established, widely documented engineering principles of FDM
        printing — thermoplastic behavior, extrusion mechanics, motion control — the kind of material covered
        consistently across printer and slicer manufacturer documentation, materials science references, and the
        broader 3D printing community's shared, tested knowledge. Where a specific claim comes from a particular
        source rather than general consensus, we link to that source directly in the article rather than
        presenting it as unattributed fact.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not claim to have personally tested every printer, filament brand, or combination of settings
        described in a guide. Where a guide would benefit from first-hand testing we haven't done, it's written to
        explain the underlying mechanism and general ranges rather than presenting an untested specific number as
        verified fact.
      </p>

      <h2>How AI is used</h2>
      <p>
        AI tools are used internally for research assistance, outlining, drafting, and editing. Every published
        article is reviewed for technical accuracy before publication, and we do not publish AI output that hasn't
        been checked against the sourcing standard above. We do not use AI to fabricate test results, personal
        experience, statistics, or sources — where we haven't verified something, we say so or leave it out.
      </p>

      <h2>Authorship</h2>
      <p>
        We don't invent author names, credentials, or biographies. Articles represent the editorial position of The
        Layer Line as a publication rather than a claimed individual expert, because that's an accurate description
        of how the content is produced.
      </p>

      <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Articles show a "Published" date and, where they've been substantively revised, an "Updated" date. We
        prioritize updates for pages covering fast-changing information (specific software features, current
        product recommendations) over pages covering stable engineering principles that don't change year to year.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you spot an error, see the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> — corrections are reviewed and, where
        warranted, the article is updated with a note of what changed.
      </p>

      <h2>Monetization and editorial independence</h2>
      <p>
        This site is monetized primarily through Google AdSense, with more detail in our
        <a href="/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a>. Advertising has no influence over which topics we cover or
        what a guide recommends. Where we ever add affiliate links, they'll only point to products actually relevant
        to the guide they appear in, and they won't change the guide's recommendations.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
